%erg 0.1
Title: Audit the merge Ticket-line requirement as friction and find a fix
Created: 2026-10-10
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-10-10T14:54Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

`skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` reads close intent from the PR body and errors when
a repo with a `tickets/` directory has none of `**Ticket:**`, `Ticket-ref:` or
`Ticket: none`. PR #1344 (permissions cleanup, no ticket) hit that error path
at merge time: the PR body had to be edited and read back before `/merge` could
run. A PR that deliberately closes nothing is common; the bounce costs a turn
and a forge round-trip each time.

Observed once, in this session. How often it recurs is unmeasured, so this
ticket does not yet claim the line is a net cost: it may also catch real
dropped close-claims (see the "Dropped close-claim sweep" in
`skills/merge/SKILL.md`).

## Work

1. Measure. Count merged PRs over a fixed window (suggest 90 days) whose body
   carries `Ticket: none`, whose first `erg-pr-merge` run bounced on the
   missing line, and which closed nothing. Sources: `gh pr list --state merged`
   bodies and edit history, session traces (`/trace-doctor`), and
   `skills/roar/check-close-claims.sh`. Report the numerator, the denominator,
   and the window; label any derived figure as derived.
2. Measure the benefit side: how many PRs in the same window had a
   missing-line bounce that revealed a real forgotten close-claim.
3. Search for fixes and price them (author-hours, risk). Candidates, not
   decisions:
   - default to "closes nothing" when the PR touches no ticket file and
     names no ticket, and keep the error only when the diff touches
     `tickets/`;
   - infer from branch or commit trailers;
   - have `erg-pr-merge` offer the edit itself (write `Ticket: none` to the
     body and read it back) instead of failing;
   - keep the requirement and have `gh pr create` templates carry the line.
4. Recommend one, with the case against it.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Measured counts and window recorded in this ticket (numerator,
      denominator, both sides of the cost).
- [ ] At least three fixes priced; one recommended with its counter-argument.
- [ ] A follow-up ticket filed for the chosen fix, or this ticket closed with
      the reason none is worth it.

Not part of this ticket: implementing the fix.
